dark:prose-invert w-full break-words light markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="159">Great — <strong data-start="8" data-end="63">your 3D model viewer is working exactly as expected</strong> ✔️<br data-start="66" data-end="69">
Let me explain both things you saw, so you understand why they happen and what to do next.</p>
<hr data-start="161" data-end="164">
<h1 data-start="166" data-end="212">✅ <strong data-start="170" data-end="212">1. Why do you see the astronaut model?</strong></h1>
<p data-start="213" data-end="370">You are currently using <strong data-start="237" data-end="259">sample model files</strong> (<code data-start="261" data-end="272">tulsi.glb</code>, <code data-start="274" data-end="284">neem.glb</code>, etc.) that I gave earlier because you have <strong data-start="329" data-end="369">not yet downloaded real plant models</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="372" data-end="448">The astronaut (or robot) is the <strong data-start="404" data-end="428">default sample model</strong> inside those files.</p>
<p data-start="450" data-end="585">➡️ <strong data-start="453" data-end="530">This means: your code is correct — but your model files are placeholders.</strong><br data-start="530" data-end="533">
